{"name":"Art Nouveau Green","hex":"#9c932f","fetched":"2026-11-01","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"art","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-5 language code for artificial languages."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The conscious production or arrangement of sounds, colours, forms, movements, or other elements in a manner that affects the senses and emotions, usually specifically the production of the beautiful in a graphic or plastic medium.","The creative and emotional expression of mental imagery, such as visual, auditory, social, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/art"},{"term":"nouveau","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["New, fashionable."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A wine released in the same year that the grapes were harvested to make it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nouveau"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Art Nouveau","blocks":[{"text":"Art Nouveau is an international style of art, architecture, and applied art, especially the decorative arts. It was often inspired by natural forms such as the sinuous curves of plants and flowers. Other characteristics of Art Nouveau were a sense of dynamism and movement, often given by asymmetry or whiplash lines, and the use of modern materials, particularly iron, glass, ceramics and later concrete, to create unusual forms and larger open spaces. It was popular between 1890 and 1910 during the Belle Époque period, and was a reaction against the academicism, eclecticism and historicism of 19th-century architecture and decorative art."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Abbesses.JPG/330px-Abbesses.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau","wikidata":"Q34636"},{"title":"Art","blocks":[{"text":"Art is a diverse range of human activity centered around works, crafted as an expression of creativity or imagination, which are expected to provide an audience with a worthwhile experience—generally, an experience of emotion, thinking, or beauty."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Art-portrait-collage_2.jpg/330px-Art-portrait-collage_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art","wikidata":"Q735"},{"title":"Nouveau","blocks":[{"text":"A nouveau, or vin (de) primeur, is a wine which may be sold in the same year in which it was harvested."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nouveau","wikidata":"Q3559383"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Abbesses.JPG/500px-Abbesses.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Abbesses.JPG/500px-Abbesses.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAbbesses.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg/500px-Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg/500px-Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOtto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Louis_comfort_tiffany%2C_lampada_da_tavolo_pomb_lily%2C_1900-10_ca..JPG/500px-Louis_comfort_tiffany%2C_lampada_da_tavolo_pomb_lily%2C_1900-10_ca..JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Louis_comfort_tiffany%2C_lampada_da_tavolo_pomb_lily%2C_1900-10_ca..JPG/500px-Louis_comfort_tiffany%2C_lampada_da_tavolo_pomb_lily%2C_1900-10_ca..JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALouis_comfort_tiffany%2C_lampada_da_tavolo_pomb_lily%2C_1900-10_ca..JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Louis_Majorelle_-_Wall_Cabinet_-_Walters_6587.jpg/50